1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,657
Annyeong — this is Liam, in for Bear.
Today's ask comes from Bear's own book on

2
00:00:04,657 --> 00:00:08,150
Living Models, chapter one: can you trust
your dashboard? Here's the uncomfortable

3
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:11,934
shape of the answer — the most expensive
analytics failures don't crash, don't

4
00:00:11,934 --> 00:00:16,300
error, don't warn. They hand you a
beautiful green line, and let you believe
it.

5
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:21,187
Act one. The chapter opens on a conference
room, a Tuesday morning, and a line

6
00:00:21,187 --> 00:00:22,490
everyone wanted to see.

7
00:00:22,490 --> 00:00:27,677
The scene: a senior data team, weekly
metrics review. On the screen, weekly
active

8
00:00:27,677 --> 00:00:32,123
users climbing eighteen percent — rendered
in a satisfying shade of green.

9
00:00:32,123 --> 00:00:36,199
Leadership leaves energized. Hiring
accelerates. The chart gets screenshot
into an

10
00:00:36,199 --> 00:00:36,940
investor deck.

11
00:00:36,940 --> 00:00:42,293
Two point one million users to two point
five. The line does exactly what a healthy

12
00:00:42,293 --> 00:00:46,642
product's line does. Nothing on this chart
— nothing — distinguishes real growth

13
00:00:46,642 --> 00:00:47,980
from what actually happened.

14
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:51,722
What happened: a partial refresh failure
quietly dropped four hundred thousand

15
00:00:51,722 --> 00:00:57,165
European user records. No error, no null,
no flag — the users just ceased to exist

16
00:00:57,165 --> 00:01:01,248
to the reporting system. The denominator
shrank; the ratio climbed. The instrument

17
00:01:01,248 --> 00:01:05,330
drifted — precisely, consistently, in the
direction that felt like good news.

18
00:01:05,330 --> 00:01:09,437
Four days passed before a routine quality
check caught it. And the chapter's

19
00:01:09,437 --> 00:01:13,860
sharpest detail: in those four days,
nobody asked whether the data was
reliable. The

20
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:18,282
number looked right, so it was treated as
right — authority borrowed from the

21
00:01:18,282 --> 00:01:19,230
appearance of precision.

22
00:01:19,230 --> 00:01:24,312
Act two. Now the same structure at
catastrophic scale — with a number that
was

23
00:01:24,312 --> 00:01:24,990
completely true.

24
00:01:24,990 --> 00:01:29,395
Twenty twelve. J.C. Penney's new CEO, Ron
Johnson, reads the transaction history:

25
00:01:29,395 --> 00:01:33,433
promotional events correlate with revenue
spikes, sluggish sales between them. His

26
00:01:33,433 --> 00:01:38,572
inference — the sales are training
customers not to pay full price. Kill the

27
00:01:38,572 --> 00:01:40,040
promotions, lift the baseline.

28
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:44,375
Pull back to what the historical record
actually contains: only worlds where the

29
00:01:44,375 --> 00:01:49,044
promotions ran. For a huge slice of
customers the promotion wasn't friction —
the

30
00:01:49,044 --> 00:01:53,046
hunt, the markdown, the win WAS the
experience. That truth was structurally

31
00:01:53,046 --> 00:01:55,380
invisible in the data. Not hidden.
Invisible.

32
00:01:55,380 --> 00:01:59,432
Eighteen months later: four point three
billion in annual revenue gone, Johnson

33
00:01:59,432 --> 00:02:04,160
fired. The chapter's scalpel — the number
was correct, the inference was wrong, and

34
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:08,212
those are different failures. Act one's
dashboard was technically false. This
number

35
00:02:08,212 --> 00:02:09,900
was technically true. Same wreck.

36
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:14,508
Watch the mechanism run. Remove the
promotions — and the baseline doesn't rise
to

37
00:02:14,508 --> 00:02:19,115
meet everyday low prices. The mechanism
customers used to choose the store is
gone,

38
00:02:19,115 --> 00:02:23,394
and revenue follows it down. No amount of
additional historical data would have

39
00:02:23,394 --> 00:02:24,710
shown this in advance.

40
00:02:24,710 --> 00:02:29,623
Act three. The chapter gives that gap a
precise mathematical name — and one small

41
00:02:29,623 --> 00:02:31,260
operator carries all of it.

42
00:02:31,260 --> 00:02:36,478
P of Y given X — the outcome given we
observe X. What co-occurs in the record. P
of

43
00:02:36,478 --> 00:02:41,971
Y given do of X — Pearl's operator — the
outcome if we reach in and set X by
action.

44
00:02:41,971 --> 00:02:45,815
Seeing a world, versus making one. Johnson
had the first. His decision needed the

45
00:02:45,815 --> 00:02:46,090
second.

46
00:02:46,090 --> 00:02:50,941
Here's what the equation commits you to:
that gap cannot be closed with more rows.

47
00:02:50,941 --> 00:02:55,792
The record holds only worlds where
promotions ran — a do steps outside every
world

48
00:02:55,792 --> 00:02:59,997
the data contains. Not a sample-size
problem. A category boundary. Back to the

49
00:02:59,997 --> 00:03:00,320
story.

50
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:05,033
This has a picture. We asked Claude to
draw the smallest graph where the
correlation

51
00:03:05,033 --> 00:03:07,860
is perfectly real — and the intervention
still fails.

52
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:12,616
Three nodes. A confounder Z drives both X
and Y — big spenders join loyalty

53
00:03:12,616 --> 00:03:17,055
programs. Observe X, and Y rides along
beautifully. Set X by force, and nothing

54
00:03:17,055 --> 00:03:22,446
moves — the arrow into Y never came from
X. One hidden parent, and seeing and doing

55
00:03:22,446 --> 00:03:23,080
come apart.

56
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:28,340
Act four. Pearl stacked these ideas into a
ladder — three rungs, and no elevator.

57
00:03:28,340 --> 00:03:32,498
Rung one, association: what does the data
show? Every dashboard lives here. Rung

58
00:03:32,498 --> 00:03:36,975
two, intervention: what happens if we act
— the do-operator's home, and where real

59
00:03:36,975 --> 00:03:40,493
decisions live. Rung three,
counterfactual: what would have happened
otherwise? The

60
00:03:40,493 --> 00:03:44,970
hardest questions of all — they need a
structural model of the mechanism itself.

61
00:03:44,970 --> 00:03:50,504
The ladder's one brutal property: no rung
is reachable by piling up more of the rung

62
00:03:50,504 --> 00:03:55,000
below. A thousand rows plus a structural
causal model answers questions a billion

63
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,497
rows plus a correlation engine cannot.
Penney didn't need more transaction data.
It

64
00:03:59,497 --> 00:04:00,880
needed a different instrument.

65
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:06,030
Which is worth sitting with, because it
runs against how a generation of data

66
00:04:06,030 --> 00:04:09,708
organizations were built. Warehouses of
history, beautifully indexed — every

67
00:04:09,708 --> 00:04:14,122
terabyte of it parked on rung one,
answering yesterday's question with
increasing

68
00:04:14,122 --> 00:04:14,490
precision.

69
00:04:14,490 --> 00:04:19,548
Act five. Name the failure mode precisely
— because it has a signature, and the

70
00:04:19,548 --> 00:04:20,560
signature is silence.

71
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:25,400
A system that crashes announces itself. A
system that quietly changes what it

72
00:04:25,400 --> 00:04:30,240
measures does not. The dashboard's silence
was mechanical — absence treated as the

73
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:34,708
absence of absence. Penney's was epistemic
— every component correct, the inference

74
00:04:34,708 --> 00:04:36,570
catastrophic. Same signature. No alarm.

75
00:04:36,570 --> 00:04:41,651
Predictive models have their own version:
concept drift. A model is a bet that

76
00:04:41,651 --> 00:04:45,280
yesterday's relationships persist. When
the world restructures, the model keeps

77
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:49,635
confidently predicting the old world's
future — unaware of its own staleness.

78
00:04:49,635 --> 00:04:51,450
Outputs continue. Reality has left.

79
00:04:51,450 --> 00:04:56,478
And when you wire rung-one outputs
directly to actions, silence gets
expensive fast.

80
00:04:56,478 --> 00:05:01,119
August first, twenty twelve. Knight
Capital deploys an automated trading
system. It

81
00:05:01,119 --> 00:05:04,600
begins executing — in error — at
algorithmic speed.

82
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:09,188
Forty-five minutes. Four hundred forty
million dollars. No human decision node
stood

83
00:05:09,188 --> 00:05:14,540
between the execution loop and the market
— the system worked exactly as designed,

84
00:05:14,540 --> 00:05:19,128
and the design had no provision for
stopping. The human-machine boundary isn't

85
00:05:19,128 --> 00:05:21,040
bureaucracy. It's the accountability
surface.

86
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:24,707
The chapter maps four maturity stages —
descriptive, diagnostic, predictive,

87
00:05:24,707 --> 00:05:30,208
prescriptive: what happened, why, what's
next, what to do. The trap in the map: an

88
00:05:30,208 --> 00:05:34,609
organization can climb all four and still
stand on causation's first rung,

89
00:05:34,609 --> 00:05:37,910
prescribing from correlations. The two
ladders are parallel climbs.

90
00:05:37,910 --> 00:05:42,640
Act six. What the chapter is building
toward — and it is not a better dashboard.

91
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:46,817
A Living Model has four properties. Causal
— recommendations are estimated do-

92
00:05:46,817 --> 00:05:51,342
effects. Counterfactual — it can price the
decision not taken. Continually updated —

93
00:05:51,342 --> 00:05:55,519
parameters live against incoming data.
Treatment-oriented — the output is a
ranked

94
00:05:55,519 --> 00:06:00,044
list of interventions. Run the checklist:
a dashboard scores zero of four. That's

95
00:06:00,044 --> 00:06:00,740
the distance.

96
00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:04,945
Underneath it all, the posture the chapter
names observability: a measurement system

97
00:06:04,945 --> 00:06:10,201
that treats its own integrity as a first-
class output. Not a bolted-on monitor — an

98
00:06:10,201 --> 00:06:14,756
instrument that tells you what's happening
to itself, and which questions the data

99
00:06:14,756 --> 00:06:18,260
can structurally answer. The integrity
panel belongs beside the metrics.

100
00:06:18,260 --> 00:06:22,643
Back to the conference room. Given what
the screen showed, growth was the sensible

101
00:06:22,643 --> 00:06:26,713
read — the failure wasn't incompetence, it
was architecture. The team trusted the

102
00:06:26,713 --> 00:06:31,409
screen showed the data, and the data held
the world. Neither was verified. Both were

103
00:06:31,409 --> 00:06:33,600
verifiable. That gap is the whole book.

104
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:37,511
Let's recap with Claude. Silent failures
don't announce themselves — the green line

105
00:06:37,511 --> 00:06:41,723
looks identical either way. A true number
can still power a false inference; seeing

106
00:06:41,723 --> 00:06:45,935
is not doing. The do-operator marks a
category boundary no pile of data crosses.

107
00:06:45,935 --> 00:06:50,447
Prescriptive is not causal — the two
ladders are parallel climbs. And the fix
is

108
00:06:50,447 --> 00:06:53,757
architectural: instruments that verify
themselves, and models built to answer
what

109
00:06:53,757 --> 00:06:54,960
happens if we act.

110
00:06:54,960 --> 00:06:58,862
Your turn. Here's the prompt, straight
from the chapter's own exercise: my team

111
00:06:58,862 --> 00:07:02,164
found a strong correlation between loyalty
program membership and average order

112
00:07:02,164 --> 00:07:05,766
value, and leadership wants to expand the
program. Construct two causal stories

113
00:07:05,766 --> 00:07:09,669
consistent with that correlation — one
where the program causes the spending, one

114
00:07:09,669 --> 00:07:13,571
where a pre-existing difference drives
both. Draw each causal graph, name the
data

115
00:07:13,571 --> 00:07:18,674
that would distinguish them, and express
the whole answer as P of Y given X versus
P

116
00:07:18,674 --> 00:07:24,377
of Y given do of X — then tell me what
that implies for the budget decision. Run
it

117
00:07:24,377 --> 00:07:29,180
on a correlation your own org is about to
act on. That's where the money is.

118
00:07:29,180 --> 00:07:32,400
The Dashboard That Lied. Liam, in for
Bear.

